#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""epson-esci -- make EPSON ESC-I "unsupported" flatbed scanners work on Linux.

Targets the class of EPSON scanners that SANE's open-source backends (epson,
epson2, epsonds) will never claim, and that instead need EPSON's proprietary
epkowa backend plus a non-free interpreter plugin, and that receive their
operating firmware from the host at run time. The Perfection V19/V39, GT-S650,
Perfection V37/V370, GT-F500/F600 and friends all behave this way.

Symptoms of this class:
  * The scanner "does not turn on": the LED blinks forever and never goes steady.
  * scanimage -L lists nothing, or only network scanners.
  * sane-find-scanner sees it as root but says "Access denied" as a user.
  * scanimage -d <device> fails with "sane_start: Invalid argument".
  * scanimage -d <device> fails with "Error during device I/O".

Commands:
  detect     List EPSON scanners attached, and what each one needs.
  explain    Print the full requirement chain for a USB id (default: attached).
  doctor     Run every check and print a verdict. Exit non-zero on problems.
  firmware   Check -- and with --repair, fix -- interpreter firmware collisions.
  reset      USB-reset a wedged scanner and print its new device name.
  fetch      Download EPSON bundles and verify checksums. Installs nothing.
  install    Install from a fetched directory. --dry-run prints the plan.

Everything is read-only except reset, firmware --repair and install.

Redistribution note: this script contains only URLs and checksums. It never
ships EPSON binaries, because EPSON's end-user licence forbids distributing
them (see README.md). Each user downloads from EPSON and accepts that licence.
"""

